{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born around 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father's assassination in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until his release in 1174. During his captivity *Imprisonment and Release* his stepbrother Bohemond III of Antioch acted as regent of Tripoli. After his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and began to consolidate his power.\n\nUpon his return Raymond III became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was a strong supporter of Raymond of Antioch and opposed the influence of Guy of Lusignan. *The Battle of Hattin and its Aftermath* Raymond III's military skills were put to the test during the Battle of Hattin where he commanded the vanguard of the Crusader army. The battle was a disastrous defeat for the Crusaders and Raymond III was one of the few leaders to escape.\n\nAfter the Battle of Hattin Raymond III retreated to Tripoli and played a key role in the defense of the city. He died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV of Tripoli. Raymond III's legacy was marked by his military prowess and his involvement in the complex politics of the Crusader states. Throughout his life he navigated the intricate web of alliances and rivalries between the different Crusader states and the Muslim states surrounding them. His actions had significant consequences for the history of the region and the fate of the C